WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.762
This video is about error analysis: the discipline of reading your agent's failures one at a time, naming what went wrong, and counting.

2
00:00:07.812 --> 00:00:08.781
It sounds slow.

3
00:00:08.831 --> 00:00:18.632
It is the fastest way anyone has found to improve an agent, and it is the part of this course that transfers to every other kind of AI work.

4
00:00:19.382 --> 00:00:26.227
Andrew Ng wrote a two-part letter in October 2025 making a point that practitioners had been making for a while.

5
00:00:26.277 --> 00:00:36.223
The teams that improve agents fastest are the ones that read their failures, and the teams that stall are the ones that guess at fixes from the final output.

6
00:00:36.273 --> 00:00:39.326
The final output tells you that something went wrong.

7
00:00:39.376 --> 00:00:44.841
Only the trace tells you where: which tool call, which argument, which retrieved passage, which instruction.

8
00:00:45.591 --> 00:00:46.994
The procedure is short.

9
00:00:47.044 --> 00:00:51.715
Pull twenty failed traces, chosen at random, not the ones that look interesting.

10
00:00:51.765 --> 00:01:00.794
For each one, read from the top until you find the first step where it went wrong, and write one sentence: what happened and why.

11
00:01:00.844 --> 00:01:02.610
Do not fix anything yet.

12
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.152
When you have twenty sentences, group them.

13
00:01:05.202 --> 00:01:13.868
Five to eight categories usually appear: wrong tool, invented argument, retrieved the wrong passage, ignored an instruction, stopped too early, kept going too long.

14
00:01:13.918 --> 00:01:14.595
Count them.

15
00:01:14.645 --> 00:01:21.132
Fix the biggest category first, because one fix there moves the pass rate more than ten fixes elsewhere.

16
00:01:21.182 --> 00:01:24.037
Re-run the golden set, and do it again.

17
00:01:24.787 --> 00:01:27.621
Here is a pattern worth knowing in advance.

18
00:01:27.671 --> 00:01:32.308
Most of the failures you find will be harness problems, not model problems.

19
00:01:32.358 --> 00:01:34.832
A tool description that misled the model.

20
00:01:34.882 --> 00:01:38.797
A tool result trimmed so hard the answer was cut off.

21
00:01:38.847 --> 00:01:41.682
A stop condition that fired a step early.

22
00:01:41.732 --> 00:01:51.055
Genuine model problems, where the context was right and the reasoning was wrong, exist, and they are rarer than people expect once the harness is fixed.

23
00:01:51.105 --> 00:01:52.497
This is good news.

24
00:01:52.547 --> 00:01:55.381
Harness problems are yours to fix, this week.

25
00:01:56.131 --> 00:02:00.940
In class you will do error analysis on traces that are not yours.

26
00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:14.770
GDPx is a benchmark released by SemiAnalysis in November 2026: 206 tasks written by partner firms across eleven domains, each run by several frontier models with their own harnesses, and graded by the firms.

27
00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.629
For one task per partner, every run is public, including the full trace.

28
00:02:19.679 --> 00:02:26.730
As of November 2026 the best model scored about 16 percent, which tells you how much room there is.

29
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.094
We read those transcripts ourselves while preparing this course.

30
00:02:30.144 --> 00:02:40.559
One finding to take with you: a final message that announced the work had been verified was no more likely to score well than one that did not.

31
00:02:40.609 --> 00:02:43.176
The claim of checking is not checking.

32
00:02:43.226 --> 00:02:46.539
You will see this in your own agents too.

33
00:02:47.289 --> 00:02:50.663
This is also what the trace of the week is for.

34
00:02:50.713 --> 00:02:56.576
Each week one team presents one trace: what the agent did, step by step, and what it taught them.

35
00:02:56.626 --> 00:03:00.311
Ten minutes, and show the actual trace, not slides about the trace.

36
00:03:00.361 --> 00:03:06.535
The best ones are failures, because a failure read carefully is a design lesson for every team in the room.

37
00:03:06.585 --> 00:03:11.826
Next video: using a model to grade a model, and how to know whether to trust it.
